Mobile App Security - OWASP and Other Ways

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.320
to Get Started

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.860
Mike: A couple of things I've already talked
about have already come off the OWASP top

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:14.150
ten list for mobile, which is a list that
myself, Jack Lanier, Zack Mannino, and a number

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:18.609
of other people in the application security
community collaborated on to put together

00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:22.070
this list of top ten risks for mobile.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.859
Pete: Yea, OWASP is a great organization.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.169
Mike: OWASP.org

00:00:27.169 --> 00:00:31.820
Pete: And highly recommended for all the geeks
out there that want to learn more security

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:36.840
in general- application security specifically.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.399
There's projects- one that covers mobile that
you mentioned, that you're involved in.

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:44.239
And a wealth of other resources and information
there.

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:46.760
So I highly recommend that geeks out there
check that out.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:53.399
Mike: So, one of the things- and it's actually
number two on the OWASP mobile top ten is

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:57.480
to- the number two risk is essentially weak
server side controls.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:03.929
So, I just consider it a pointer to the original
OWASP top ten for web applications, which

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:12.370
was a widely accepted list of standards- standard
risks and vulnerabilities in web applications.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:20.860
It's something that other compliance organizations
have taken to- for example, PCI- to be PCI

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:27.710
compliant, you need to be scanned and assessed
by the OWASP top ten list.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:35.399
So, when we're writing these new mobile applications,
we need to remember that it's not just code

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:37.189
going on the client anymore.

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:44.710
It's potentially a whole new server side infrastructure
and either a SOAP based web service or RESTful

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.229
service.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:52.280
And, these new pieces of technology are ripe
for attack.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:58.039
It's funny, when the iPad's first came out,
within the first couple of days there was

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:07.020
the first "Pad hack" where all sorts of private
details and data about the initial customers

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.390
who received their iPads was made public.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.960
It was essentially publicly available on the
internet.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.590
And the reason for this was, essentially,
poor server side controls.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:22.100
There was no access control on these web services
that the iPad was communicating with.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:27.301
So, people could just bypass the iPad, fire
up a web browser, talk directly to the web

00:02:27.301 --> 00:02:29.090
service and extract all this data.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.110
And that was labeled as an iPad hack, when
really it's kind of the same thing we've been

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.230
dealing with for well over a decade now.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:42.120
SQL injection, info validation attacks, improper
authorization, lack of access control.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.250
Pete: So, if I'm not starting from scratch-
what if I'm a developer and I already have

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:52.470
a mobile application out and maybe I didn't
think through some of these architectural

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.280
or sensitive data storage on the- maybe I
didn't think through all those issues when

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.930
I was originally architecting my app.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:03.930
What's the first thing I should do to try
to secure an existing mobile application?

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:04.930
Mike: Sure.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:14.100
I would perform a code review for security,
where someone looks at the code, line by line,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:20.590
manually, and assesses that code based on
the current OWASP top ten risks for mobile

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:21.590
apps.

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:26.510
Pete: So, I could do this myself as a developer,
it's not necessary to hire and expensive security

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:27.510
consultant.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:32.860
I could go to the OWASP and search or work
my way through the top ten and do a code review

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.480
of my own application for that.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.480
Mike: Certainly.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.250
I mean, something as simple as data storage
on the device- you know if you're storing

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.020
sensitive data on the SD card.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:48.520
If you see yourself doing that, that's bad
because that's basically a public location

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.860
on the device and the application could access
it.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:57.540
So, yea, I think it's certainly doable for
a developer to start working through these

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.670
risks and analyzing their own code.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.390
